title: Reconcile rewritten branches
status: implemented
created: 2026-11-22
author: Luke Carrier
context: "`Litterbox` pairs a sandbox with a Git branch. The branch is `litterbox/<name>`, a normal Git branch the user can check out and push. The workspace holds a copy of its content. Only a snapshot writes Git. It builds a tree from the workspace and commits it with the branch tip as parent (`vcs/git/scm.rs:722-821`).\n\nNothing compares the branch with the revision the sandbox last agreed on. A user who amends, squashes or force-pushes the branch is reverted by the next snapshot. The commit tree comes from the workspace, which predates the rewrite, so a file the user changed reverts as well (`scm.rs:758-790`).\n\nThe contract already names the operations. `Vcs` declares `identity`, `tip`, `set_tip`, `synced`, `set_synced`, `merge`, `conflicts`, `conflict_sides`, `resolve`, `abort`, `history` and `restore` (`vcs/mod.rs:101-137`). `GitVcs` implements `identity` and `tip`. The rest return `unsupported` (`scm.rs:558-614`). Rewritten branches need `synced` and `set_synced`, a tree identity for a revision and a tree identity for the workspace, and a snapshot that writes the sandbox branch without moving the shared branch.\n\nThe map `maps/2026-11-18-litterbox-vcs-history-sync` settles the design. T-007 records the axes, the classification and the plan table. T-008 records the three refs. T-004 proves that a three-way merge produces a usable tree.\n\nThis ADR keeps the two branches apart. The sandbox keeps its own branch at `refs/litterbox/workspaces/<name>`. It adopts a rewrite when it has no unsaved work. When it has unsaved work it leaves the shared branch at the user tip and reports the divergence. The user resolves the difference on the host. The sandbox adopts the result at its next operation.\n\nThe host merge needs a base Git cannot infer. After a rebase the merge base of the two tips lies below the revision the sandbox last saw. `refs/litterbox/synced/<name>` records the base both sides agreed on, and `git merge-tree --merge-base` takes it as an argument.\n\nStaging writes to a linked worktree under the data directory and never to the user's checkout. A person runs the command. The MCP tool stages only and its availability is the harness's decision. Finishing is a command line action. The workflow is documented in FR-017.\n\nAdoption reports the revision it drops. The bare store logs every ref update so the branch reflog keeps that revision for the reflog lifetime. No new ref is minted.\n\n{{mermaid: plan.mmd}}"
problem: "The branch is written only by the sandbox. A user who rewrites it is reverted by the next snapshot because that snapshot commits the old workspace content on top of the rewrite. Nothing records the revision both sides agreed on, so nothing separates a moved history from changed content. Nothing keeps the sandbox's own work when the branch moves. Nothing gives the user a conflict to resolve on the base the two sides share."
goals[6]:
  - "A rewrite is visible at the next operation before the tool runs."
  - "No snapshot commits a tree that undoes a rewrite."
  - "The sandbox's unsaved work survives a rewrite on either side."
  - "Divergence reaches the user with the base both sides agreed on."
  - "No tool is refused."
  - "Nothing writes the user's checkout without consent."
nonGoals[5]:
  - "Conflict mechanics in the sandbox: markers, `resolve` and `abort`."
  - "Staging as a side effect of a sandbox operation."
  - "jj. `Native` conflicts belong to that backend."
  - "History retention and restore."
  - "Lifecycle: pause, resume, repair and delete."
requirements[18]{id,title,slug,description}:
  FR-001,"The source state",source-state,"The orchestrator owns a state with three axes. `Base` is `Synced` or `HistoryMoved` or `ContentMoved`. `Workspace` is `Clean` or `Dirty`. `Merge` is `None` or `Pending` or `Conflicted`. `Base` and `Workspace` are computed at each operation. `Merge` is read from the record. For Git `Pending` means the branch moved while the sandbox had unsaved work. Git never writes `Conflicted`."
  FR-002,"Tree identity",tree-identity,"`Base` and `Workspace` come from four trees. `synced_tree` is the root tree of the synced revision. `branch_tree` is the root tree of the branch tip. `sandbox_tree` is the root tree of the sandbox branch tip. `workspace_tree` is the tree a snapshot would commit. `Base` is `Synced` when the branch tip is the synced revision. It is `HistoryMoved` when the tip differs and `branch_tree` equals `synced_tree`. It is `ContentMoved` otherwise. `Workspace` is `Clean` when `workspace_tree` equals `sandbox_tree` and `Dirty` otherwise. Comparing the workspace with the sandbox branch tells the sandbox whether the agent has unsaved work. Comparing it with `synced` would misread a branch the user already reconciled. Ancestry alone never decides."
  FR-003,"Observing the workspace",observing-the-workspace,"`workspace_tree` is built from the sandbox's current content through the filter a snapshot applies. The observation writes no commit and moves no ref. It happens when the plan needs it and never on a timer. The pre-plan defers it when the branch has not moved and the action is not `Snapshot` and passes `Clean`. An empty workspace is the empty tree. A missing ref is initialised first (FR-012)."
  FR-004,"The plan",the-plan,"One function takes the state and the action and the conflict model and returns ordered steps. It is the only code that reads the axes. With merge `None` and base `Synced` a clean workspace gives `Proceed` and a dirty one gives `Snapshot`. With base `HistoryMoved` a clean workspace gives `AdoptHistory` and a dirty one gives `AdoptHistory` then `Snapshot`. With base `ContentMoved` a clean workspace gives `AdoptContent` and a dirty one gives `Block`. With merge `Pending` a tool gives `Proceed` and a snapshot gives `Snapshot` on the sandbox branch. A repair gives `Materialise`. A backend whose conflicts live in commits can return `Merge` or `Resolve` or `Abort`. Git returns none of them. The function performs no I/O."
  FR-005,"Lazy planning",lazy-planning,"Every operation that touches the workspace plans before it acts inside the sandbox lock. A mutating tool call plans then runs the steps then runs the tool then observes the workspace then plans again for `Snapshot`. The state an operation reports is the state after the second plan. A read operation runs the same first plan so that an adoption is visible before the tool reads. No watcher runs and no timer runs. No command triggers a sync."
  FR-006,"No tool is refused",no-tool-refused,"`Block` does not refuse a tool. The tool runs and its edits stay in the workspace. `Block` stops the branch advance and records `Pending`. The conflict model is `Blocking` for Git. There is no `Deferred` state and there are no notes."
  FR-007,"Adopt history",adopt-history,"When the branch moved without changing its tree the plan sets `synced` to the branch tip. The workspace and the sandbox branch are untouched. No commit is created. A squash or a rebase that leaves the tree identical lands here."
  FR-008,"Adopt content",adopt-content,"When the branch tree differs and the workspace is clean the plan replaces the workspace with the branch tree. It moves the sandbox branch and `synced` to the branch tip. The replacement covers the paths a snapshot records so ignored files the sandbox created stay. No VCS metadata is written. It reports the old sandbox tip when the new tip does not contain it. It does not mint a ref for the old tip."
  FR-009,"Snapshot",snapshot,"A snapshot commits `workspace_tree` on the sandbox branch. When the branch is not held it also advances the branch and sets `synced` to the new commit. An unchanged tree creates no commit. The commit is a compare-and-swap against the tip the plan read. If the tip moved the step is abandoned and the operation plans again a bounded number of times."
  FR-010,"Divergence",divergence,"When the branch moved and the workspace is dirty the plan blocks the advance. It records `Pending` with the branch tip and the sandbox tip and the synced revision. The branch stays at the user tip. The workspace stays as the agent left it. The result shows the user's diff and says the branch is held. The report names the paths that would conflict. Snapshots continue on the sandbox branch. Git does not merge. While `Pending` the plan tests whether the sandbox branch tip is an ancestor of the branch tip. When it is the user has merged the sandbox work so the plan clears `Pending` and classifies again."
  FR-011,"The three refs",three-refs,"`GitVcs` keeps three refs. `refs/heads/litterbox/<name>` is the branch. `refs/litterbox/workspaces/<name>` is the sandbox branch and the `ours` side. `refs/litterbox/synced/<name>` is the revision both sides last agreed on. The refs live in the repository that holds the branch. Under `direct` that is the user's repository. Under `remote` it is the bare store and the exposed remote carries `refs/litterbox/workspaces/*` in its fetch refspec. The user can reach the sandbox branch without Litterbox. The bare store logs every ref update (`core.logAllRefUpdates=always`) so a dropped tip stays in the branch reflog for the reflog lifetime. Under `direct` the user's repository logs branches by default."
  FR-012,"Creation and removal",creation-and-removal,"`prepare` creates all three refs at the base revision. A record whose refs predate this change is initialised on first use from the branch tip. No record is rewritten. `delete_branch` removes all three. A sandbox whose branch is gone is `NotFound`."
  FR-013,"The recorded base",recorded-base,"`synced` is the base of the host merge. Git's inferred merge base is not usable after a rewrite. A rebase replaces the commits the sandbox branch descends from so the merge base of the two tips lies below the revision both sides last saw. The host commands pass `synced` to the merge."
  FR-014,"The report",report,"A report names the sandbox and `synced` and the branch tip and the sandbox tip and the paths that would conflict. It names the branch reflog that holds the previous tips and the command that lists them. It computes the merge with `synced` as the base. It moves no ref and writes no file in a checkout. It names the staging worktree when one exists."
  FR-015,"The staging worktree",staging-worktree,"A staging command creates a linked worktree under the data directory on a temporary branch at the branch tip. It merges the sandbox branch with `synced` as the base and stops before committing. The worktree holds marker files for the conflicted paths and index stages for each side and `MERGE_HEAD`. The command prints the worktree path and the conflicting paths. It refuses when a staging worktree already exists for the sandbox."
  FR-016,"Consent",consent,"No sandbox operation stages a worktree. The command runs only when a person invokes it. The MCP tool exposes the same staging and does nothing else. Whether an agent may call the tool is the harness's permission decision. Finishing has no MCP equivalent. No command writes the user's checkout."
  FR-017,"The workflow",workflow,"Stage. Resolve in the worktree and commit. Finish checks that the worktree is clean and that the merge commit descends from the branch tip. When no worktree holds `litterbox/<name>` finish fast-forwards the branch to the merge commit then removes the worktree and deletes the temporary branch. When the user's checkout holds the branch finish prints `git merge --ff-only <commit>` and leaves the worktree and the branch in place. A later finish lands the cleanup. Abort removes the worktree and leaves the branch untouched. The record stays `Pending` until the branch contains the sandbox tip."
  FR-018,"The backend writes",backend-writes,"The orchestrator names no ref. The Git backend owns every ref write. The contract gains a tree identity for a revision and a tree identity for the sandbox branch and a tree identity for the workspace. It separates the sandbox branch from the branch advance. No Git type and no ref string crosses the contract."
nonFunctional[5]{id,title,slug,description}:
  NFR-001,"Pure and total",pure-and-total,"The plan is a total function of its three arguments. It performs no I/O and takes no lock and reads no clock. A table test covers every combination."
  NFR-002,"No revert",no-revert,"No path commits the workspace tree on a tip the plan did not read. A tip that moves under an operation triggers a new plan and a bounded retry. When the retries run out the operation fails and names both tips. The branch is untouched."
  NFR-003,"Local",local,"Reconciliation never uses the network. The bare store is local so a push is visible without a fetch. A plan execution observes the workspace at most once. A tool call observes once on the common path and twice when the branch moved. Each step writes once. No state is kept in memory between operations."
  NFR-004,"No new dependency",no-new-dependency,"`git2` stays in `vcs::git_common` and `vcs::git`. `arch-lint check` and `bash hack/arch-lint-negative.sh` pass unchanged."
  NFR-005,"No hooks",no-hooks,"Every Git command on the staging path runs with `core.hooksPath` pointing at an empty directory so no repository hook executes. The staging path writes only inside the worktree it created and inside the repository's worktree metadata."
acceptance[10]{id,title,slug,description}:
  AC-001,"A squash adopts history",squash-adopts-history,"Rewrite `litterbox/<name>` with a squash that leaves the tree identical. The next operation sets `refs/litterbox/synced/<name>` to the new tip. No commit is created and the workspace bytes do not change."
  AC-002,"A content rewrite is adopted",content-rewrite-adopted,"Rewrite the branch with a different tree while the workspace is clean. The next operation's tool sees the rewritten content. The sandbox branch and `synced` equal the branch tip."
  AC-003,"Divergence holds the branch",divergence-holds,"Change a file in the sandbox and rewrite the branch so the trees differ. The branch stays at the user tip. The record says `Pending`. The result shows the user's diff. The report names the conflicting paths. The tool still runs."
  AC-004,"The report uses the recorded base",report-base,"Rebase the branch so its merge base with the sandbox tip lies below `synced`. The reported conflict list matches a merge with `synced` as the base and not a merge with Git's inferred base."
  AC-005,"No tool is refused",no-refusal,"A tool call runs in every state including `Pending` and reports its own result. The only thing that stops is the branch advance."
  AC-006,"Both localities",both-localities,"The three refs exist in the user's repository under `direct` and in the bare store under `remote`. A force-push is adopted at the next operation in both modes."
  AC-007,"Staging leaves the checkout alone",staging-isolated,"Stage a diverged sandbox. The user's checkout is unchanged. The worktree holds markers and index stages and `MERGE_HEAD`. The command prints the worktree path and the conflicting paths."
  AC-008,"Finish lands the merge",finish-lands,"Resolve in the worktree and commit. Finish fast-forwards `litterbox/<name>` to the merge commit and removes the worktree. The next operation adopts because the sandbox tip is an ancestor of the branch."
  AC-009,"Abort leaves the branch",abort-leaves,"Remove the worktree without finishing. The branch stays at the user tip. The record stays `Pending`."
  AC-010,"The gates pass",gates-pass,"`cargo test -p agentkit-litterbox`; `cargo clippy -p agentkit-litterbox --all-targets -- -D warnings`; `arch-lint check`; and `bash hack/arch-lint-negative.sh` pass."
edgeCases[12]{id,title,slug,description}:
  EC-001,"A legacy record",legacy-record,"A record written before this change has no `synced` and no sandbox ref. Both are initialised to the branch tip on first use. A sandbox that was rewritten before the refs existed is not recovered. The initialisation is logged."
  EC-002,"A branch that is gone",branch-gone,"A sandbox whose branch was deleted is `NotFound`. The operation fails and creates no ref."
  EC-003,"An empty workspace",empty-workspace,"An empty workspace is the empty tree. It is clean only when the sandbox branch is empty."
  EC-004,"Repeated rewrites",repeated-rewrites,"Two rewrites between operations are observed as one. Only the latest tip is compared."
  EC-005,"Ignored files survive",ignored-files-survive,"Adoption replaces only the paths a snapshot records. Build outputs and `.env` files the sandbox created stay."
  EC-006,"A dirty checkout does not block staging",dirty-checkout,"Staging works while the user's checkout is dirty because it writes only to its own worktree."
  EC-007,"A remote without the refspec",missing-refspec,"When the exposed remote has no fetch refspec for `refs/litterbox/workspaces/*` the report names the missing refspec. It does not show an empty diff."
  EC-008,"A snapshot after the host merge",snapshot-after-merge,"When the sandbox snapshots after the user merged `Pending` stays because the new sandbox tip is not an ancestor of the branch. The user merges the new tip before the sandbox adopts."
  EC-009,"A second stage",second-stage,"A second staging command for the same sandbox refuses and names the existing worktree path."
  EC-010,"A dirty worktree at finish",dirty-worktree,"Finish refuses when the worktree has uncommitted changes or unresolved paths. It leaves the worktree and the branch alone."
  EC-011,"A stale worktree",stale-worktree,"A worktree whose directory is gone is pruned before the next stage."
  EC-012,"A dropped tip stays in the reflog",reflog-keeps,"After adoption drops a sandbox tip the reflog of the branch in the store lists it and the report names the branch reflog. No new ref exists for it."
